@page "/ai/scenarios/approval"
@inherits AppComponentBase

<PageOutlet Url="ai/scenarios/approval"
            Title="AI tool approval"
            Keywords="human in the loop, tool approval UI, destructive tool confirmation, BitAiToolApproval, approval policy, agent safety"
            Description="A destructive tool that stops and waits for a human in a bit BlazorUI AI chat: allow once, allow always, or decline, with the turn genuinely paused while it waits - whether the app's approval policy or the provider asks for the decision." />

<DocsArticle Eyebrow="AI scenarios"
             Title="Tool approval"
             Lead="A destructive tool stops and waits for a human. Allow once, allow always, or decline - and a provider can ask for the same decision itself."
             GitHubUrl="AI/Scenarios/Approval/AiApprovalScenarioPage.razor">

    <Badges>
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI" />
        <BitTag Size="BitSize.Small" Variant="BitVariant.Outline" Color="BitColor.SecondaryForeground" Text="Scripted transport" />
    </Badges>

    <ChildContent>
        <DocSection Id="demo" Title="Live demo"
                    Description="Ask for something destructive: delete_records is declared destructive, so the session will not run it unattended.">
            <DemoExample Title="A call that waits for a decision" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example1CodeFiles" Id="example1">
                <AiScenarioFrame>
                    <BitAiChat Session="_session"
                               EmptyTitle="Ask for something destructive"
                               EmptyBody="delete_records is declared destructive, so the session will not run it unattended."
                               Suggestions="_suggestions" />
                </AiScenarioFrame>
            </DemoExample>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="transport-approval" Title="Approval the transport asks for"
                    Description="Ask about the weather: get_weather is read-only, so this app's policy would run it unattended - but this transport asks for the user's consent itself, and says why.">
            <DemoExample Title="A decision the transport asks for" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example2CodeFiles" Id="example2">
                <AiScenarioFrame>
                    <BitAiChat Session="_transportSession"
                               EmptyTitle="Ask about the weather"
                               EmptyBody="The transport asks for consent before get_weather runs, whatever the app's policy says."
                               Suggestions="_transportSuggestions" />
                </AiScenarioFrame>
            </DemoExample>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="how-it-works" Title="How it works">
            <p>
                The transport asks for the tool as any tool call is asked for (<code>BitAiToolCallStartedEvent</code>, the
                arguments streamed as <code>BitAiToolCallDeltaEvent</code>s, <code>BitAiToolCallCompletedEvent</code>) and
                ends the reply with <code>BitAiFinishReason.ToolCalls</code>. Before the session runs the call, it asks the
                approval policy (<code>IBitAiToolApprovalPolicy.RequiresApprovalAsync(definition, invocation)</code>). When a
                decision is needed it moves the call to <code>AwaitingApproval</code> and raises
                <code>BitAiChatSession.ApprovalRequested</code>, and the call's card shows the prompt, whose buttons record
                the decision with <code>BitAiChatSession.Approve(toolCallId, decision)</code> - <code>Approved</code>,
                <code>ApprovedAlways</code> or <code>Rejected</code>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Allowing once and allowing always are different decisions. Collapsing them into a single button forces the
                user either to re-approve constantly or to grant more than they meant to, so both are offered, beside a
                decline.
            </p>
            <p>
                What needs a decision is the <code>IBitAiToolApprovalPolicy</code>'s call. The default one asks for every
                tool whose <code>Risk</code> is <code>Destructive</code> (or that says <code>RequiresApproval</code>) and
                remembers the tools a user allowed always - which is why it is scoped on Blazor Server, where a singleton
                would carry one user's "always" over to every other user. <code>ai.UseApprovalPolicy&lt;T&gt;()</code>
                replaces it, for a decision that depends on the arguments or on the user.
            </p>
            <p>
                While a call waits, the turn is genuinely paused: the tool has not run, the model has not been asked again,
                and stopping the turn cancels the wait. A declined call is marked <code>Rejected</code> and given a failed
                result, which the transport reads off the call on the next request, so the model can explain that nothing
                was done.
            </p>
            <p>
                A provider whose own policy wants consent asks for it with a <code>BitAiToolApprovalRequestedEvent</code>,
                as the second demo's transport does right after starting the call. It puts the call in
                <code>AwaitingApproval</code> with its <code>Reason</code>, which the prompt shows as the question, and the
                session waits for the user just as it would for its own policy - even for a tool that policy runs
                unattended, and whatever the user allowed always before: the request is the provider's, not the policy's
                to overrule. The session still runs the tool once it is allowed, so the tool is registered as usual.
            </p>
        </DocSection>

        <DocSection Id="components" Title="Components used">
            <ul>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolapproval"><code>BitAiToolApproval</code></BitLink> - the prompt: allow once, allow always, or decline.</li>
                <li><BitLink Href="/components/ai/toolcallview"><code>BitAiToolCallView</code></BitLink> - the card the prompt sits in.</li>
            </ul>
        </DocSection>
    </ChildContent>

    <FooterTemplate>
        <DocPager Pages="AiScenarioNav.Items" AriaLabel="AI scenarios" />
    </FooterTemplate>
</DocsArticle>
